the city's longest serving battalion  chief is  preparing to retire next week lawrence  wycliffe has served the las vegas valley  for nearly 50 years  reporter kate houston sat down with him  to look back at his career  i've had some of the largest highway  incidents  largest configuration type fires after  46 years  las vegas fire battalion chief lawrence  wickliffe is about to retire  wycliff started in the mid 70s when he  decided to enroll in training  freshly out of college this was his  first profession and he was determined  to go far  when the fire chief interviewed me when  i first got hired he said  where do you see yourself going in the  fire service i said  your chair throughout his career he saw  the department go through many changes  when i got hired women were not on the  job  and i guess i became a woman's advocate  as one of very few african americans on  the job  he made an impact on the community i've  had people  literally come up in the middle of a  fire  that i was incident commander they  wanted to know  if i was a firefighter because they  never seen a black  guy running a fire or in charge  throughout his career he's responded to  emergencies that put las vegas into the  international spotlight  such as one october the pep con  explosion and mgm  fire i was out in front of the building  just starting ivs and  patient assessment without even going  through  the hospital codes because it was just  too many people  after nearly five decades of service he  says he's most proud of the men and  women he worked with in his battalion  and his ability to provide for his  family but i would like to see  us as a group or an organization be the  uniting  factor to help solve some of our  country's  differences because it's a profession  that takes care of you and care for you  and it has nothing to do with race  has nothing to do with gender it's all  about  you as a human being kate houston 8 news  now
 

